From a593c960d17558ac6e0b99781040e1416041f155 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:05:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 147/152] dns: the fallback asks the provider of every uplink
 through it; a list for each box

The fallback took the DNS servers of one device: the one sing-box binds its
sockets to, else the one of the default route with the lowest metric. With
mwan3 the WAN often stays up while its provider is down: mwan3 sends the
traffic through the backup modem, the default route of the main table is
still the WAN's, and the fallback asked the WAN's provider, now through the
modem, where providers answer only their own customers. It answered nothing
until the list was read again, at least 30 s later.

When sing-box binds no socket, the list holds the servers of every uplink (an
interface that is up, has a default route and is no tunnel), and a query to
a server of an uplink is bound to the device of that uplink: each provider is
asked through its own line, whatever mwan3 does. The answers choose the
server as before: an uplink whose provider is down does not answer, the one
that comes back takes over when it answers first. When sing-box binds its
sockets to a device, the servers of that device alone, as before.

The list belonged to the process: a box that replaced another one in the
same process (SIGHUP, the Go backend of podkop) with another output device
asked the servers of the old one. The DNS router of each box keeps its own.
